#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Critical Points of Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b1d1a2e-5cdf-5c6a-ace8-818954c38398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283073} {:answer/id #uuid "4d92fd6d-c367-5859-8da2-c1a317efa08a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283074} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2def8a-cf94-57a8-997e-089a9c22b797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283075} {:answer/id #uuid "78a9a3dd-c88a-5730-8af8-49e3e561841a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283076} {:answer/id #uuid "b84874f0-d9f9-5284-b498-0d91ad0d6cea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b84874f0-d9f9-5284-b498-0d91ad0d6cea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283077} :answer-field/id #uuid "65add198-edef-57ec-9f82-e394376b4295" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c236be5c-f7ce-577e-8440-1e61c10bf0dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-127390" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4039e1a7-cc37-51a3-ab37-2c71b8952690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2\\pm \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283080} {:answer/id #uuid "8630c70d-ec2e-5494-9ac9-e24c2e41a159" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283081} {:answer/id #uuid "f3695305-1cab-5741-bf86-c34271fd5d71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\pm \\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283082} {:answer/id #uuid "95225043-0ced-530d-8ce3-c6518410fa26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2\\pm \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283083} {:answer/id #uuid "21d24e56-c1ed-50ff-85e3-f1206e4334ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\pm \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21d24e56-c1ed-50ff-85e3-f1206e4334ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\pm \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283084} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf076dfe-9ae6-539c-81af-8a6891189af3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "020c0b00-cb4a-56fb-b3c8-84c40dc80072" :question/math-academy-id "q-132464" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+6}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{2}+6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)-(x-2)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+6)-(x-2)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+6-2{x}^{2}+4x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+4x+6}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x-6}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x-6}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-6 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-4-6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}-10 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{10} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=2\\pm \\sqrt{10}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=2\\pm \\sqrt{10}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cda348-b3e8-5a89-abd4-bd6f3a7a8370" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Critical Points of Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32d641ba-ee11-5d27-9896-21a463702ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32d641ba-ee11-5d27-9896-21a463702ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283087} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d76069f-db56-5e1b-94b3-d7570368376d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283086} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d691ab7-c99c-5721-b50e-52cbeb66846f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d691ab7-c99c-5721-b50e-52cbeb66846f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283089} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9b41e47-202c-53ce-a2c4-9acccf3e190f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "400c8835-a3d5-5c89-91f7-0b22f3f1a5d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-345505" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ In **ascending order**, the **approximate values**, rounded to **two decimal places**, of the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-6 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-6)+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x≈-1.41$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x≈1.74$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx≈-1.41,\\,x≈1.74.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x≈-1.41$ and $x≈1.74.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-1.41\\,\\text{and}\\,x=1.74\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15aaf551-bf34-542d-9fc6-91318a842944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283092} {:answer/id #uuid "6afad7fb-8207-5b5a-9568-8270188b9f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283093} {:answer/id #uuid "e31e3fa9-5215-5d6b-b073-dbaa4c626589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283094} {:answer/id #uuid "91145809-13a5-5277-9068-c7c7d1186a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283095} {:answer/id #uuid "15619ebb-7757-52f1-954c-c8317bb330f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15aaf551-bf34-542d-9fc6-91318a842944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283092} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b1e0662-f7c3-5743-b6d9-4a287a964e1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f7d83bc-e215-56af-9039-1143690e2108" :question/math-academy-id "q-48596" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2})}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-6x){e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-6x){e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x & =0 \\\\ 3x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=0$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cedfade-ee75-59e1-ba37-303041bc54f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283099} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d7b048-b736-5f00-8f74-3a2a1f611307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283100} {:answer/id #uuid "d268f45b-b3c1-52bd-b251-00bf5f5b3da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283101} {:answer/id #uuid "5c0624be-188f-505f-8d9a-3a6e3bd93f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283102} {:answer/id #uuid "9a05ffc8-d328-590c-8251-8b19351f45c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c0624be-188f-505f-8d9a-3a6e3bd93f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283102} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb7aac87-d32c-5242-bdef-f3accb76fdb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb13e83e-9cd0-53cb-aaf0-21f542a77f1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99050" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function  $f(x)={e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & ={(12x-{x}^{3})}^{′}{e}^{12x-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =(12-3{x}^{2}){e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12-3{x}^{2}){e}^{12x-{x}^{3}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince ${e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(4-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (2-x)(2+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-2$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$"}]}
